Large vision-language models (LVLMs) have shown strong reasoning capabilities by leveraging Large language models (LLMs). However, hallucination remains a challenge for many LVLMs. Through our experiments, we identify that existing noise in shallow layers often leads to unstable token predictions during reasoning. We find that visual token pruning technologies along with multiplicative decoding effectively mitigate this interference. Building on this insight, we propose Adaptive Layer-wise Denoising Decoding (ALD
), a novel framework that mitigates hallucinations within LVLMs by dynamically selecting an optimal shallow layer for visual-token pruning and multiplicatively fusing the denoised distributions with that of the final layer. Extensive experiments on several benchmarks (CHAIR with 500 images; POPE with 500 images; MME with 2374 cases) demonstrate that ALD
 consistently improves the accuracy across multiple LVLMs. For example, we achieve an average accuracy improvement of 5.61% on POPE metrics using the InstructBlip-Vicuna-7B model and an increase of 88.56 MME score points in the MME evaluation benchmark with the LLaVA-Next-8B model. Code available at: https://github.com/Yangmrl-nlp/Adaptive-Layer-wise-Denoising-Decoding.翻译

